Falsifies `capability::the_view_and_the_reap_have_the_same_scope`.

The defect: a live child of this very rendezvous is answered `NotSupervised`
instead of `StillAlive`, by folding the liveness test into the match guard. It
reads as a simplification (one arm instead of two, and `StillAlive` becomes dead
code) and it is the drift milestone 126 built one predicate to prevent: `ps` still
lists a running child, and a `REAP` of that same child now answers as though it
belonged to somebody else.

Expected to fail: the harness's only assertion,
`assert_eq!(survey_includes(fault_ep, invoked), reap_decision(fault_ep, invoked,
dead) != Reap::NotSupervised)`, on any `fault_ep == Some(invoked)` with `dead`
false. Confirmed: the harness goes red under this patch and green without it.

**What was predicted here and measured false, because that is the whole point of
running it.** The first draft of this note claimed the defect was caught by this
harness alone. Measured against all four supervision harnesses, it also turns
`reap_is_permitted_only_to_the_supervising_rendezvous` red, through that harness's
`NotSupervised => assert_ne!(fault_ep, Some(invoked))` arm: the live own-child now
lands in `NotSupervised` while naming the invoked rendezvous. The two harnesses it
does leave green are `a_stranger_reveals_nothing_about_its_liveness` (a stranger
still answers identically at both liveness values) and
`a_survey_shows_exactly_the_endpoints_own_children` (`survey_includes` is
untouched). So this patch is evidence that the assertion is wired to the
predicate, and it is not evidence that the harness discriminates: the honest
statement of that is in notes/falsification.md's own BUGS, that a recorded
falsification proves the harness catches THAT defect and not the class.

